# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 3 c), with its solution sheet.
id: conditional-min
title: Fill in the precondition of a conditional (c)
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 3 c
tags: conditional, wp

task:
  Fill in the weakest precondition of the following program.

pre: x = y
post: z <= y && z <= x

program:
  {R3}
  if (x - y > 0) then {
    {R1}
    z := x            // then
  } else {
    {R2}
    z := y            // else
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The then branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(z := x, Q)
  = (z <= y && z <= x)[z <- x]
  = x <= y && x <= x
  = x <= y
set: R1 = x <= y

step: The else branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(z := y, Q)
  = (z <= y && z <= x)[z <- y]
  = y <= y && y <= x
  = y <= x
set: R2 = y <= x

step: Combine the branches
rule: IF, WP
focus: if
calc:
  wp(if (x - y > 0) then z := x else z := y, Q)
  = (x - y > 0 ==> x <= y) && (!(x - y > 0) ==> y <= x)
  = (x > y ==> x <= y) && (x <= y ==> y <= x)
  = x <= y && (x <= y ==> y <= x)    // A ==> !A is the same as !A
  = x <= y && y <= x                 // A && (A ==> B) is A && B
  = x = y
set: R3 = x = y
text:
  The first implication says: if $x > y$ then $x <= y$. Its two sides
  contradict each other, so it can only hold when $x > y$ is false, that
  is, when $x <= y$.
result: The weakest precondition is $x = y$.

summary:
  The program computes the maximum of $x$ and $y$, but the postcondition
  describes the minimum. The two agree only when $x = y$, and this is what
  the weakest precondition says.
